{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"C1: Forgetting the grouping convention","title_html":"C1: Forgetting the grouping convention","html":"<p>Suppose we forgot rule 4 of the shorthand, so that <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"p \\to q \\to r \\to s\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45D TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2192\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45E TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2192\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45F TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2192\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D460 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>q</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>s</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> could stand for any\nofficial formula obtained by inserting parentheses into it.</p>\n<div class=\"enumerate\" data-options=\"label=(\\alph*)\">\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>List every official formula it could stand for, and draw the shape\nof each parse tree.</li>\n<li>How many are there? Say how the count is found, by splitting at the\nmain connective.</li>\n<li>Which of them is the one rule 4 chooses?</li>\n</ol>\n</div>","file":"ch03-semantics-soundness-completeness/01-formulas-and-parse-trees.html"}